\f4\fs26 It was only 3.30am when the alarm broke into song, but Jameson Hardy was already awake and had commenced his morning rituals. Sycophants and sundry minions had been scurrying about for some time now, preparing breakfast, clothing options and a bathing facilities for wealthiest man in all of Ulyssia. Finding his way to the wet-room, he sprightly stepped into the glass booth, not unlike an old shower recess, where a halo of bright radiant colour appeared from above and slowly lowered down over his head, down his body to the floor before it popped out of existence. \
\'93Zero pathogens detected, General Health at 90% optimum. Have a good day Mr Hardy.\'94 The computer voice of the scanner, an exact replication of his own mother\'92s verbal patterns, soothed to the country\'92s foremost citizen and left him swimming in happy childhood memories.\
 His enormous claw-foot bath was already drawn and gleaming in the centre of the bathroom, steam rising enticingly from the clear, still water. Standing silently, he entered slowly, testing the temperature with toes, then calves, thighs and after some considerable time his backside. After a few minutes of complete silence, the only he would enjoy for the entire day, he hit the remote, bringing to life a wall of beeping technology and comms-devices. The colossal array of holographs, vid screens and computer monitors linked him with every market, finance institution, insurance house and brokerage concern on the planet. Jamison had his fingers in as many pies and if there was a school-kid in the Hindu-Kush selling his pulao at lunch time, Jamison wanted in on the action.\
His first order of business was wading through the list of vid-mails that inevitably lay in wait. Vid-mail took much longer to view and action than text as most messages were peppered with irrelevancy and unnecessary emotion. Although it was more time consuming, Jameson found that he was better able to judge character; not in spite of the irrelevant bullshit, but because of it. Unconscious affectations of voice and motion revealed more about the speaker to Jameson than the contents of the message itself. Scanning the vast list of his in box, his attention was immediately drawn to the 10 messages from Professor Hermann, the head lab-coat from his Bio-Weapons Division. The professor never made contact unless it was something of interest, unlike most department heads who only ever called to bleat about funding.\
The first message was short and cryptic. \'93Eureka\'94 the face on the main screen had said. He played the next message \'96 \'93Eureka\'94 the professor said once more. All messages were the same \'96 \'93Eureka.\'94 \
\'93Okay, I\'92m intrigued.\'94 Jameson said, jumping out of his bath and heading for his office, grabbing only a robe for the sake of appearances and leaving behind retinue of butlers and sycophants holding a variety of garments in the air. He could have quite easily have remained in the bath and made the call, but vid-calling from the bath was just plain impolite and he was nothing if not polite. 
\f5\i Smile to their faces and stab them in the back.
\f4\i0 \
The professor was still in his lab when Jamison called at 5.00am. \'93What\'92s with the messages, Professor?\'94 \
\'93I think you should come and see me here. I have had some remarkable results. Remarkable.\'94 He was beaming with delight.\
\'93I don\'92t have the time. Just give me a report.\'94\
\'93No, not over interlink lines. I need to see you now.\'94\
\'93This phone is hardwired and encrypted. It cannot be hacked. I simply cannot drop everything for you Professor Hermann. If you can\'92t give me a report, stop wasting my time.\'94 Jameson said, not entirely amused.\
\'93I am not trying to upset you Mr. Chairman, but this will change human existence. We have manufactured the virus.\'94 \
 \
\'93A Professor of Virology has found a virus, well done. Why don\'92t you contact the boys in Oncology and see if they have found any cancer lately? You\'92ll be able to exchange war stories.\'94\
\'93No. Not a virus, the virus.\'94 The professor said proudly, assuming the tycoon would connect the dots.\
\'93The virus, huh? If you don\'92t start making sense soon, I am going to pull your funding.\'94 Jameson knew this would get him talking.\
\'93It targets and kills all known human pathogens. The TPlusVirus is an actual panacea. \'94\
\'93Shit, do you know what this means?\'94 Jamison didn\'92t really know, but he did not think it would be good for business.\
\'93A world without disease,\'94 his faced beaming with pride.\
